## ROW 1: BUILD KS-1897 (TIER 1; to READY FOR GATE)
**State of the base (drafter, live read 18:38 AEDT; you re-read).**
- develop `9bb6c98b2c47228e2c217cff30f252e1f2148177` (`gh api repos/Secuura/Distributed_Secuura/branches/develop` AND `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/develop` in the project's `2_Project_Files`, both equal at 18:35 and again 18:38; GH_CONFIG_DIR=`/Volumes/DevMASTER/!CODING/Secuura/Blockchain/4_Credentials/.gh-config`, account kksecura). It CONTAINS PR 2232 (KS-1934, merged, merge commit `96aa5fc17c60`) and PR 2220 (merged, `9bb6c98b2c47`). Build on it: NO stack, no stacked PR.
- Billing tree at that develop (blob shas, so you can prove "nothing moved under you"): routes/webhooks.ts `8e641e04c1cf`, routes/billing.ts `7098e91112e2`, services/stripeService.ts `300f02c4d292`, creditGrants.ts `d1b5b7f574d3`. Billing suite on develop: 21 files, 395 tests (gate 361's count, rc 0, twice).
- **There is NO `creditLock.ts` on develop** (it is PR 2235's new file). `grantCreditsOnce` therefore calls `addCertificationCredits` unlocked on develop and locked once 2235 merges; your code does not care which.

**What develop does today (read at 9bb6c98b2c47; line numbers are THAT blob's).** `routes/webhooks.ts` `handleCheckoutComplete` (492): `customerId = session.customer` (498); `if (!customerId) { logger.error('No customer ID in checkout session'); return 0; }` (503-506); then PR 2232's `payment_status !== 'paid'` check (513) logging `CHECKOUT_NOT_PAID_MSG`; then `if (certifications > 0)` -> `grantCreditsOnce({ paymentKey: checkoutPaymentKey(session), customerId, credits, eventId, eventType }, (c,n) => stripeService.addCertificationCredits(c,n))` (521-526). The dispatcher (378-388) routes BOTH `checkout.session.completed` and `checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded` through it. `handlePaymentSucceeded` (555) credits only when `paymentIntent.customer` is set. `routes/billing.ts`: `POST /checkout/bundle` (465) and `/checkout/custom` (511) read `(req as any).user?.stripeCustomerId` (477, 521; assigned nowhere in services/ or packages/ at A 31st's census) and pass `customerEmail: data.customerEmail` from the BODY (zod 178, 185: `z.string().email().optional()`; passed at 481, 525). The builders (`stripeService.ts` 287 and 352) spread `params.metadata` into the session metadata AFTER their own keys (330, 383: `bundleId`, `certifications`/`type`, then `...params.metadata`) and set `customer_email = params.customerEmail` only when no `customerId` (342-343, 395-396). `listCustomersByEmail` (stripeService.ts 204: `customers.list({ email, limit: 100 })`, no pagination) already exists. The owner rule is `mayActOnCustomer` (billing.ts 127, NOT exported): platform-role pass, then `metadata.userId === caller.userId` (both non-empty strings), and where the customer records `metadata.tenantId` it must equal the caller's.

**The design (Kam's b-ii, restated; Seat A 31st's plan is the long form).**
1. *Record the buyer at checkout.* In `POST /checkout/bundle` and `/checkout/custom`, pass `metadata: { userId, tenantId }` into the builders, taken from the authenticated token (`req.user.userId`, `req.user.tenantId`), NEVER the body. No stripeService.ts change is needed (the builders already spread `params.metadata`). Only string, non-empty values; if the token has no `userId`, send no metadata (that session matches nothing later). Note the spread order: `...params.metadata` comes LAST, so a metadata key named `certifications` or `bundleId` would override the price metadata: send exactly `userId` and `tenantId`, and add a cell that the route sends no other key (and that a body field cannot reach metadata).
2. *The match, in `handleCheckoutComplete`, ORDER (fixed by Kam and Seat A 31st): (i) the paid check FIRST (unpaid: no lookup, no claim); (ii) `certifications > 0` (else the existing warn, no lookup); (iii) customer, or the match; (iv) `grantCreditsOnce`.* This moves PR 2232's paid check ABOVE the old no-customer return and moves the no-customer decision below `certifications > 0`. Keep PR 2232's line exactly, `logger.error('No customer ID in checkout session')` + `return 0`, for a session with NEITHER `session.customer` NOR an email (so cell C2d in `ks1934-checkout-session-credits-only-when-paid.test.ts` stays true and that file is NOT edited; its C2d fixture has `customer = null` and no `customer_details`/`customer_email`, test lines 282-291; the file's stripe double has no `listCustomersByEmail`, so every cell there must still pass without it being called). MEASURE that the ordering change keeps ks1934's 11 other cells green (they all carry a customer).
3. *The email.* `session.customer_details?.email ?? session.customer_email` (the drafter agrees with Seat A 31st: `customer_details.email` is what Stripe collected, equal to `customer_email` when that was prefilled; billing reads neither today; neither identifies the buyer; it is only the lookup key). Case: Stripe's `customers.list({email})` is documented exact and case-sensitive; that is NOT measured (see UNMEASURED); do not lower-case, do not invent a normaliser.
4. *Keep only the buyer's own customers.* `listCustomersByEmail(email)`, then keep those where `customer.metadata.userId === session.metadata.userId` (both non-empty) AND, where the customer records a `tenantId`, it equals `session.metadata.tenantId` (an absent session tenantId then fails a recorded one, exactly as `mayActOnCustomer` does with an absent caller tenant). This is `mayActOnCustomer`'s owner+tenant rule WITHOUT the platform-admin pass. Do not call the exported-nowhere function: either export it for reuse or write a tiny pure helper in a NEW file in `services/billing/src/` imported by `webhooks.ts` ONLY (a new file is in your write set and keeps `billing.ts` free of new imports; see the 2235 overlap below). Decide, record the reason in the PR. A session with no `metadata.userId` (every session created before this change) matches nothing.
5. *Result.* Exactly one left: credit it through `grantCreditsOnce` (key `checkoutPaymentKey(session)`, as today). ZERO left: credit nothing, answer 200, log `sessionId` and a reason only (no email, no customer id: PII) and write nothing to `billing_credit_grants` (the return precedes `grantCreditsOnce`, so the admin replay `POST /webhooks/replay`, webhooks.ts 297-321, credits it exactly once after the buyer's customer exists). TWO OR MORE left: credit nothing, log `sessionId` and the COUNT, answer 200 (default; "oldest owned" is a money choice nobody ruled). Never "first in the list".
6. *Unchanged, stated residuals (put them in the PR, plainly).* (a) `payment_intent.succeeded` gets NO match: it credits only a PaymentIntent with `customer`, so for an email-only purchase it credits nothing and claims nothing; order against the session event is safe both ways. (b) Whoever pays a session credits the person who created it (a forwarded payment link). (c) If the credit call throws and a redelivery arrives after the buyer's customer set changed, the NEW customer is credited while the claim row still names the first (`CLAIM_GRANT_SQL` rewrites status/event_id/event_type, not `customer_id`): one credit, wrong record; under b-ii only among the buyer's OWN customers; creditGrants.ts is NOT touched. (d) More than 100 same-email customers: the list is cut. (e) The `webhook_events` row for a matched credit records `customer_id` null (the dispatcher sets `result.customerId` from `session.customer`, webhooks.ts 382) with `credits_added` > 0; A 31st's candidate filing (b), the reconciliation list, must therefore filter on `credits_added = 0`, not on null customer alone: say so in your HANDOVER; do not change the dispatcher unless a cell needs it. (f) A paid email-only session by a buyer with no customer is left uncredited until someone creates the customer and replays: Kam ruled "credit nothing"; the create-a-customer alternative is NOT built. (g) No frontend calls the checkout routes (A 31st's census: BillingDashboard.tsx:470 posts to `/api/billing/checkout`, which billing does not serve): candidate filing, not yours.
7. *No spec change.* No body field is added: `billing.openapi.ts` and `docs/openapi` stay untouched. If you find you need one, that is a QUESTION (the regenerated spec is outside your glob).

**Cells (red first on develop `9bb6c98b2c47`, then green; a fake Stripe client and a fake db; NO Stripe call of any kind; HOLDS).** A NEW test file `services/billing/src/__tests__/ks1897-checkout-email-only-credits-only-the-buyers-own-customer.test.ts` (name is a suggestion). Model the stripe double on ks1934's (`vi.mock('../services/stripeService')`, `addCertificationCredits`, `constructWebhookEvent`) plus a `listCustomersByEmail` (or `customers.list`) double recording its arguments. Minimum cells, each a real assertion on grants/balance and on what was logged:
- *M1 the matched buyer:* a paid session, no customer, email E, `metadata.userId` U; one customer under E with `metadata.userId` U: 1 credit to THAT customer, once (redelivery adds 0).
- *M2 the squatted customer (the point of the PR):* the only customer under E is owned by X (`userId` X), session buyer U: 0 credits, 200, no grant row. **Plus the planted AS-WORDED MUTANT**: a copy of the handler that matches on email alone (Kam's rejected (c)); the cell must be RED against it (it would credit X), and you run it and record it in evidence. State plainly in the PR that the mutant is a test-only plant, not shipped.
- *M3 two matches:* two customers owned by U under E: 0 credits, a log with the count, no email in any log line.
- *M4 mixed:* X's squatted customer AND U's own under E: exactly U's is credited (X's never), once.
- *M5 no recorded buyer:* a session with an email and NO `metadata.userId`: 0 credits, the double's `customers.list` NOT called (or called and ignored: pick, assert).
- *M6 tenant:* a customer of U that records tenant T1 and a session tenant T2: not credited; a customer with no recorded tenant: credited.
- *M7 order:* an UNPAID session with email and buyer: the lookup double is NOT called and no grant row is claimed (KS-1934 trap i); `certifications` 0 or missing: lookup NOT called.
- *M8 neither:* no customer, no email: the exact PR 2232 line `No customer ID in checkout session` logged once, 0 grants (this is C2d's behaviour; do not edit the ks1934 file).
- *M9 async path:* `checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded` with an email-only session credits the matched buyer (KS-1934 ii: the delayed-method purchase neither path credited); and `completed` then `async_payment_succeeded` for one session credits ONCE (one payment key).
- *M10 customer-bound unchanged:* a session WITH `customer` credits it and the lookup double is NOT called.
- *M11 the route half:* `POST /checkout/bundle` and `/checkout/custom` with an authenticated token: the builder double receives `metadata` equal to exactly `{ userId, tenantId }` from the TOKEN, not from the body (send a body `userId`/`tenantId`/`metadata`: they must not appear; zod strips unknown keys but PROVE it); a token with no `userId`: no metadata. Look at how ks445/ks1888 drive the routes (`call('POST', '/checkout/custom', ...)`, the builder mocked as `createCustomCheckoutSession`) and reuse that harness.
- *M12 a rejected lookup:* `listCustomersByEmail` throws: what does the handler do? The dispatcher's existing failure path (ks488 fail-closed idempotency, webhooks.ts) must still apply: the event is not marked processed and Stripe redelivers; assert the real behaviour, do not invent it.
Mutants (against the WHOLE billing suite, per changed line, each restored and sha256-asserted): paid check moved after the match; owner filter dropped (= the as-worded mutant); `=== 1` changed to `>= 1`/`first`; tenant check dropped; email logged; metadata taken from `req.body`; route sends extra keys. A SURVIVOR is a finding in your READY, not a footnote. The billing suite on develop FIRST (395 tests, 21 files; stop if it is not green), then with your change (395 + yours, rc 0), interleaved twice.

**Write set.** `Blockchain/Dev/services/billing/src/**` (routes/webhooks.ts `handleCheckoutComplete`; routes/billing.ts the two checkout routes; at most one NEW helper file; your new test) + a KS-1897 fragment pair `Projects Documents/fragments/{flow,cheat}/KS-1897-<slug>.html` (copy the shape of the KS-1934/KS-1935 pairs; run `node Blockchain/Dev/scripts/docs-fragments.mjs check` green, then a planted defect rc 1, restored by content; Seat A 31st's trap c: the flow fragment may have two `<!--n-->.` markers). NOT in the write set: `stripeService.ts`, `creditGrants.ts`, `billing.openapi.ts`, `docs/openapi/**`, `ks1934-*.test.ts`, anything outside billing.

